faq:
  - q: "How do you reach out to a recruiter on LinkedIn?"
    a: "Three components: who you are (title, current or most recent company, 1–2 outcome metrics), what you are looking for (specific role type, level, location preference), and a clear ask (20-minute call, or simply asking if they have relevant roles). Keep it under 150 words. Avoid 'I am open to new opportunities' without context — recruiters receive hundreds of these. Give them exactly what they need to know if you are a fit."
  - q: "Should I reach out to in-house recruiters or agency recruiters?"
    a: "Both — for different purposes. In-house recruiters at your target companies can put you in their pipeline for current and upcoming roles at that specific company. Agency recruiters (contingency and retained) work across multiple clients and can surface roles you would not find on your own — particularly at VP+ levels where retained search is common. Prioritize in-house recruiters for specific target companies and agency recruiters for broader market access."
  - q: "What should I say when a recruiter reaches out to me?"
    a: "Even if you are not actively looking, respond: 'Thank you for reaching out. I am not actively searching right now, but I am always open to hearing about [role type] opportunities — especially at [company type]. What does the role involve, and what is the compensation range?' This response keeps the relationship alive, signals your target, and gets the range without committing to anything."
  - q: "How do I follow up with a recruiter who has gone quiet?"
    a: "One professional follow-up after 5–7 business days with no response: 'Hi [Name], following up on my message from [date]. I remain interested in connecting — particularly if you have [role type] opportunities in [location/remote]. Happy to share more about my background if helpful.' If there is still no response after two attempts, move on. Persistence in recruiter outreach means volume of new contacts, not repeated pushes on unresponsive ones."
  - q: "What is the difference between a contingency recruiter and a retained recruiter?"
    a: "Contingency recruiters are paid when they place a candidate — they work on multiple roles at once and send candidates to open requisitions. Retained recruiters (executive search) are paid a retainer upfront to conduct a dedicated search — they work exclusively on one search at a time and handle VP, SVP, C-suite, and Board placements. For roles above $200K, retained search firms are a major channel that most candidates never access directly."
